# Photovoltaic Cell Efficiency Study

## Solar Irradiance

panel_area = 1.6
irradiance = 1000
solar_input = panel_area * irradiance

## Cell Parameters

### Open-Circuit Voltage

open_circuit_v = 0.72
short_circuit_i = 9.5

### Fill Factor

The *fill factor* represents how close the cell's **I-V curve** approaches the ***ideal*** rectangular shape.

fill_factor = 0.78

## Power Output

max_power = open_circuit_v * short_circuit_i * fill_factor

## Efficiency

efficiency = max_power / solar_input * 100

> The theoretical maximum efficiency for a single-junction silicon cell
> is approximately 33.7% (the *Shockley-Queisser limit*).

## Temperature Dependence

The efficiency decreases by approximately **0.5%** per degree Celsius above 25C.

base_temp = 25
operating_temp = 45
temp_coefficient = 0.005
temp_loss = (operating_temp - base_temp) * temp_coefficient
adjusted_efficiency = efficiency * (1 - temp_loss)

## Data Sample

Measured voltages at different light intensities:

```
Intensity (W/m2)  |  Voltage (V)
200               |  0.65
400               |  0.68
600               |  0.70
800               |  0.71
1000              |  0.72
```

## Array Configuration

### Series Connection

modules_in_series = 12
string_voltage = open_circuit_v * modules_in_series

### Parallel Strings

parallel_strings = 4
total_current = short_circuit_i * parallel_strings

### Total Array Output

array_power = string_voltage * total_current * fill_factor

## References

For more on photovoltaics, see [NREL](https://www.nrel.gov/pv/) and related research.

An image of a typical I-V curve:

![I-V Curve](iv-curve-diagram.png)

<https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar> provides additional resources.

## Summary

The panel achieves an efficiency of roughly **5%** under standard conditions, with a temperature-adjusted value lower at operating conditions. This analysis uses *basic* electrical parameters and does **not** account for shading, soiling, or inverter losses.
